    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let me try to explain it in more physical terms.\n\nIn quantum entanglement, when two particles are entangled, their properties become correlated in a way that can't be explained by classical physics. This means that if you measure the state of one particle, the state of the other particle is instantly affected, regardless of the distance between them.\n\nThink of it like a game of \"Quantum Entanglement Tag\". Imagine two particles, A and B, that are entangled in such a way that their spin properties are correlated. If you measure the spin of particle A, you'll get a certain outcome, say \"up\". Immediately, the spin of particle B will be correlated with the outcome of A, so if you measure B, you'll get \"down\". This correlation happens even if the particles are separated by large distances, like billions of kilometers.\n\nNow, the \"spooky action\" part comes from the fact that this correlation happens instantaneously, faster than the speed of light. This seems to defy classical notions of space and time, and it's what Einstein called \"spooky action at a distance\".\n\nIn a real physical sense, entanglement is often explained by",
